<RCC>
  <!--
    Paths here point at WORKING COPIES under assets/interface/ and
    assets/branding/, named for the control they drive. The untouched design
    package is assets/source/original-design-package/ and nothing embeds from
    it directly; assets/README.md states the rule and why.

    ONE icon source. Before spec phase 2 there were three packages in the tree:
    assets/icons/ (app icon), assets/Interface/ (first-pass player icons) and
    the 260807 package, which supersedes both and says so in its own
    CONTENTS.txt. Both of the others are gone. The first-pass set was never
    actually needed as a separate directory, because the approved package
    carries a byte-identical copy of it as player-icons/, labelled SUPERSEDED
    and kept for reference; it supplied the two frame-step glyphs, and as of
    spec phase 5 neither of those is embedded any more.
  -->
  <qresource prefix="/icons">
    <file alias="trace-16.png">../assets/branding/app-icon/png/windows/trace-16.png</file>
    <file alias="trace-32.png">../assets/branding/app-icon/png/windows/trace-32.png</file>
    <file alias="trace-48.png">../assets/branding/app-icon/png/windows/trace-48.png</file>
    <file alias="trace-256.png">../assets/branding/app-icon/png/windows/trace-256.png</file>
  </qresource>

  <!--
    Transport icons. Only controls that exist in the app are embedded; the rest
    of the base UI set (inspector, zoom, rotate, loop, ...) is deliberately left
    out until those features are real. `volume` left that list at UI redesign
    roadmap step 5, which gave Mute and Loop visible controls. Spec phase 8 is the
    first time assets/interface/common/ is not empty: the Share menu is real, so
    its three item glyphs arrived with it, and share_menu became the transport
    button's artwork under the name of the control it drives.

    The approved set ships 24px @1x and 48px @2x. It has no @3x, so a 3x display
    scales the 48px master rather than picking a dedicated file. Nothing here
    has a -72 any more: the only two glyphs that did were the frame-step pair
    from the superseded first-pass set, and both are gone. SVG masters sit
    beside each PNG in assets/interface/ if that ever matters, but using them
    needs Qt6::Svg, which Trace does not link.

    ARTWORK FOLLOWS BEHAVIOUR, ONE CONTROL AT A TIME. The approved package
    contains no frame-step icon by design ("Left/Right Arrow handle single
    frames"); its transport_scan_* pair is the artwork for the redesigned Rewind
    and Fast-forward. Shipping scan artwork over stepping behaviour would put a
    lie on a visible control in a player whose third pillar is that it can be
    trusted, so each glyph moved in the commit that changed what its button
    does, neither before it nor after it.

    Spec phase 4 did the forward half: the old Next Frame button became
    Fast-forward, so next-frame left the tree with it. Phase 5 did the backward
    half and took prev-frame. Frame stepping is the arrow keys and has no
    button, so this list is now exactly the controls that exist.

    rewind/fast-forward ARE transport_scan_reverse/transport_scan_forward,
    renamed to what they do.
  -->
  <qresource prefix="/ui">
    <file alias="play-24.png">../assets/interface/transport/play-24.png</file>
    <file alias="play-48.png">../assets/interface/transport/play-48.png</file>

    <file alias="pause-24.png">../assets/interface/transport/pause-24.png</file>
    <file alias="pause-48.png">../assets/interface/transport/pause-48.png</file>

    <file alias="fullscreen-24.png">../assets/interface/window/fullscreen-enter-24.png</file>
    <file alias="fullscreen-48.png">../assets/interface/window/fullscreen-enter-48.png</file>

    <file alias="exit-fullscreen-24.png">../assets/interface/window/fullscreen-exit-24.png</file>
    <file alias="exit-fullscreen-48.png">../assets/interface/window/fullscreen-exit-48.png</file>

    <!-- In use from spec phase 4: the forward transport control and the
         composited overlay's right region. -->
    <file alias="fast-forward-24.png">../assets/interface/transport/fast-forward-24.png</file>
    <file alias="fast-forward-48.png">../assets/interface/transport/fast-forward-48.png</file>

    <!-- In use from spec phase 5: the backward transport control and the
         composited overlay's left region. -->
    <file alias="rewind-24.png">../assets/interface/transport/rewind-24.png</file>
    <file alias="rewind-48.png">../assets/interface/transport/rewind-48.png</file>

    <!-- In use from UI redesign roadmap step 5. go-to-start and go-to-end
         (authored here at step 5, accepted "for now") LEFT with their buttons
         at owner item 15, 2026-08-18: artwork follows behaviour, in both
         directions. Home and End remain the keyboard route.

         The volume glyph set is the Trace_AudioSlider package's (2026-08-20,
         designer option 1b), which superseded the step 5 pair: the 260817
         package's volume master and the volume-muted authored beside it. The
         step 5 decision "mute button, no volume slider" was REVERSED by the
         owner on tester feedback the same day, and the inline slider ships behind
         TRACE_VOLUME_SLIDER (0 restores the mute-only button exactly). -->
    <file alias="volume-24.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-24.png</file>
    <file alias="volume-48.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-48.png</file>

    <file alias="volume-muted-24.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-muted-24.png</file>
    <file alias="volume-muted-48.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-muted-48.png</file>

    <!-- The expanded state of the volume control (the Trace_AudioSlider package,
         option 1b, 2026-08-20): speaker only, waves replaced by the inline
         slider the strip draws beside it. All three volume glyphs are that
         package's; the earlier volume/volume-muted pair was superseded by this
         delivery. The slider itself is code-drawn (track/fill/thumb from the
         timeline's own cells) so it ships no PNG; the package says so. -->
    <file alias="volume-open-24.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-open-24.png</file>
    <file alias="volume-open-48.png">../assets/interface/common/volume-open-48.png</file>

    <!-- Loop, the design package's own master. It sat in source/ from the
         2026-08-17 swap because the strip had no loop button, which was the
         "artwork follows behaviour" rule holding; the button arrived at UI
         redesign roadmap step 5 and the glyph came with it, in the same commit,
         which is the same rule the other way round. -->
    <file alias="loop-24.png">../assets/interface/common/loop-24.png</file>
    <file alias="loop-48.png">../assets/interface/common/loop-48.png</file>

    <!-- In use from spec phase 8: the transport Share button, on the docked bar
         and in the composited overlay's right region. This is share_menu from
         the approved package, renamed to the control it drives. -->
    <file alias="share-24.png">../assets/interface/transport/share-24.png</file>
    <file alias="share-48.png">../assets/interface/transport/share-48.png</file>

    <!-- Share MENU ITEMS, not transport controls, which is why they are in
         common/ rather than transport/. copy-lucidlink is a NEUTRAL CHAIN
         GLYPH and not a LucidLink brand mark; the package says so, and it may
         only be replaced with a licensed official asset. It is embedded even
         though its command cannot run until phase 9, because the design's state
         semantics require the row to be shown-and-unavailable rather than
         hidden. -->
    <file alias="copy-path-24.png">../assets/interface/common/copy-path-24.png</file>
    <file alias="copy-path-48.png">../assets/interface/common/copy-path-48.png</file>

    <file alias="show-in-explorer-24.png">../assets/interface/common/show-in-explorer-24.png</file>
    <file alias="show-in-explorer-48.png">../assets/interface/common/show-in-explorer-48.png</file>

    <file alias="copy-lucidlink-24.png">../assets/interface/common/copy-lucidlink-24.png</file>
    <file alias="copy-lucidlink-48.png">../assets/interface/common/copy-lucidlink-48.png</file>

    <!-- The empty state's mark, roadmap step 3, LEFT on 2026-08-21 with the
         owner's decision to ship its idle animation. Neither animated property
         (the edge gradient's rotation, the glow's hue cycle) is reachable from
         a bitmap, so the mark is drawn procedurally in OverlayModel from
         assets/interface/branding/empty-mark.svg, and renditions beside it
         would be embedded-and-unused. Artwork follows behaviour: the PNGs went
         out with the code that read them, in the same commit, and the SVG
         master stays because it is the specification the paths were authored
         from. -->

    <!-- The top chrome's brand mark (a 15/30 pair of trace-play-mark.svg)
         LEFT with the strip's mark and wordmark: owner item 1's approved
         interim, 2026-08-18. While the native title bar remains, the app
         identity appeared twice and the strip's copy was the redundant one.
         It returns with roadmap step 12, the frameless window, when the strip
         becomes the only header. The master is the delivered package's own
         trace-play-mark.svg, so nothing is lost by the working copy leaving;
         note it is a DIFFERENT asset from empty-mark.svg (scale, edge stops,
         stroke, glow, specular) and rendering the wrong one looks almost
         right. -->

  </qresource>
</RCC>
